const en:Copy={title:"How to research a LoloBuy order before you buy.",intro:"A practical, independent guide based only on public LoloBuy statements and on checks every buyer can make.",back:"Back to home",open:"Open FindSpreadsheet",sections:[
["Start with verifiable facts","LoloBuy’s public homepage describes a service that assists with buying products from Chinese online and offline channels. It says visitors can paste a product link to start an order and that goods are sent to its China warehouse. Those facts explain a starting flow; they do not prove stock, final price, delivery time or customs results for an individual order."],
["Open the original listing","A spreadsheet is for discovery, not a substitute for the live product page. Record the exact title, chosen colour, size or variant, displayed price, seller and date checked. Similar photos can refer to different sellers, versions or size charts. Re-open the current listing before you decide."],
["Use the link as a reference","A pasted link gives the service a product reference. It is not a promise that the seller still has every option or that the order needs no clarification. Write down the exact option you want and the reason you would reject it. For clothing, compare measurements; for electronics, check plugs, voltage and included accessories."],
["Separate listed price and total cost","The price on a listing is not automatically the final cost. Product cost, domestic movement, service options, international shipping and destination charges can be separate. Avoid universal shipping-price claims. Set an all-in budget with room for change; if the order only works when every estimate is exact, it is not ready."],
["Use the warehouse stage as a checkpoint","LoloBuy says goods go to its China warehouse. That stage separates the seller’s dispatch from international delivery. Keep the original page, chosen variant and order notes together. If images or details become available, compare them to your own acceptance criteria. Visible images help comparison but cannot prove fit, material, durability, authenticity or later delivery."],
["Keep a research record","A useful spreadsheet row includes the original URL, product name, variant, price and currency, seller, date checked, measurement notes, visible-photo notes and a status. Add a clear risk column for unclear sizing, conflicting descriptions, only promotional imagery or unusual prices. This stops copied links becoming unexamined recommendations."],
["Ask narrow questions","Instead of asking whether an item is “good”, ask what can be checked: material, measurements, selected option, included accessory or current availability. Save the answer and date beside the order. One answer is not a permanent rule for every listing, seller, parcel weight or destination."],
["Do not promise outcomes","No independent guide can promise a delivery date, customs result, duty amount or authenticity. Those outcomes depend on the parcel, destination, carrier, local rules and events outside this guide. Use conditional wording—check, compare, confirm and allow for change—rather than guarantees."],
["Make the next click useful","FindSpreadsheet is a place to continue product research, not a claim that it sells, verifies or guarantees every item. Search with concrete features such as measurements, material, plug type or dimensions. Then use the current source listing to make the final comparison."],
["How to compare two possible listings","When two listings look alike, do not compare only the headline price. Put the exact selected option next to each other: measurements, material wording, seller feedback available on the source page, visible images, stated accessories and return or change information currently shown by the seller. Mark what is missing instead of silently assuming that it is the same. A lower price can be reasonable, but it can also reflect a different version, a different unit of sale or a missing accessory. The goal is not to find a universal “best link”; it is to create a small, evidence-led shortlist that another person could audit. If the facts cannot be compared, the listing is not yet ready to recommend."],
["Keep evidence current and explain uncertainty","Product pages, stock status, selected options and quoted services can change without a spreadsheet being updated immediately. Add a date to every useful finding and revisit important rows before submission. If a photograph is promotional rather than product-specific, say so. If a measurement is absent, say so. If a final charge or delivery point cannot be known until later, label it as an estimate rather than presenting a confident number. This approach may sound less exciting than a list of promises, but it is much more useful to a buyer and more credible for search. Clear uncertainty is a form of practical guidance: it tells the reader exactly what needs to be confirmed next."],
["Plan the order as a sequence of decisions","A useful purchasing plan has small decision points rather than one large leap of trust. First decide whether the live listing meets your product requirement. Next decide whether the selected variant, measurement or specification is unambiguous. Then decide what information would make you stop: a different colour, missing accessory, unsuitable measurement or an unexplained price change. Only after those points are clear should you look at the order flow. The warehouse stage can then be used as a later evidence check rather than as a substitute for doing the first product research. This sequence makes it easier to stop early when the facts no longer support the purchase."],
["Keep claims proportionate to the evidence","A search page earns trust when its language matches the strength of its evidence. A current listing can support a statement about its displayed name, option, price or image. A public platform homepage can support a statement about the service it describes. Neither source, by itself, supports broad promises about every seller, every parcel, every country or a future delivery result. For that reason, this guide avoids labels such as guaranteed, safest, cheapest or fastest. They may generate clicks, but they do not help a reader make a reliable decision. Specific observations with dates are more valuable than generic praise, particularly when a reader must spend money after leaving the page."],
["Use a realistic budget and exit rule","Before you submit a link, decide what maximum total you can accept and what change would make you exit the order. Do not use an attractive product price to erase uncertainty about later charges. Keep a simple record of the displayed product cost, your own budget ceiling and the questions still unresolved. If a later quote or product detail exceeds that ceiling, you have already made the decision rule and do not need to rationalise it in the moment. This is not a claim about any particular platform charge; it is a practical habit for any assisted purchase. A good spreadsheet supports this habit by showing facts and open questions next to each other."],
["Why this method is useful","The point of this guide is not to make every purchase look risky or difficult. It is to give the buyer a calm order of operations: verify the listing, record the chosen option, distinguish known facts from estimates, and decide what evidence is needed next. That structure is useful whether you are comparing one item or building a longer shortlist. It also makes a later change easier to understand because the original facts and decision rule are visible."],
["Final checklist","Before submitting a link, confirm that you opened the current listing, recorded the exact variant, understand that listed price is not necessarily final cost, have a clear acceptance rule and will re-check evidence at the warehouse stage if available. Source checked: LoloBuy public homepage, 3 September 2026. This guide is independent and does not process orders or payments."]
],faq:[
["What does LoloBuy say it does?","Its public homepage describes assistance with purchasing products from Chinese online and offline channels."],["Can I start with a product link?","The homepage says a visitor can paste a product link to start an order. Confirm the current listing first."],["Where are goods sent?","The homepage says goods are shipped to the LoloBuy China warehouse; that is not final-destination delivery."],["Does a link guarantee availability?","No. Stock, options, seller response and listing details can change."],["Is a displayed price the final cost?","Not necessarily. Product, shipping, service and destination charges can be separate."],["Can this site promise delivery or customs results?","No. Those depend on the parcel, destination, carrier and local rules."],["Do images prove quality or authenticity?","No. They can show visible details but cannot guarantee fit, material, durability, authenticity or delivery."],["Does this guide process orders or payments?","No. It is an independent research guide."],["What should I save before ordering?","Save the URL, title, selected variant, price, seller, date, measurements and open questions."],["Where can I continue research?","Use FindSpreadsheet, then check the current source listing before deciding."]]};
const refreshedEnglishSections:Copy["sections"]=[
["What LoloBuy publicly says — and what it does not say","Before comparing products, separate the platform’s own description from everything a buyer still needs to check. LoloBuy’s public homepage says it assists with purchases from Chinese online and offline channels. It says a visitor can paste a product link to start an order, and says goods are sent to its China warehouse. Those statements explain a possible order flow. They do not establish that a particular seller has stock, that a displayed option is still available, that an item matches its photographs, or what a final landed cost will be. This distinction is not negative; it is the starting point for a better decision. Treat platform statements as context and live product evidence as the thing you must verify before you spend."],
["Start with the live listing, not a saved recommendation","A spreadsheet, social post or search result can help you discover a product, but it cannot freeze a live listing in time. Open the original page and look at the title, seller, selected colour or variant, displayed price and date. Save those details in one row before you compare anything else. Two listings can reuse the same image while offering different versions, units of sale or size charts. A familiar product name is not enough. If you cannot describe exactly what you are choosing, you are not ready to submit a link. This is the most useful habit in assisted buying because it turns a vague find into a record that can be reviewed later."],
["Treat the pasted link as a reference, not a guarantee","The homepage’s link instruction is useful, but it should be read literally. A link tells the service which product you want reviewed or ordered; it does not guarantee that every variation remains available or that no question will arise. Write a short selection note before you continue: the exact variant, the required feature, and the reason you would refuse it. For clothing, use measurements rather than only a letter size. For electronics, check plug format, voltage, storage or included accessories. For any product, identify what visible evidence would be enough for you and what uncertainty you are unwilling to accept."],
["Build the budget around uncertainty, not only the headline price","A listed product price is one observation, not a promise of the final amount. Product cost, movement within China, selected service options, international transport and charges at the destination can be separate parts of an order. Their treatment can vary with the parcel, route and destination. That is why a single universal shipping price or duty figure is not responsible guidance. Decide your maximum total before you place a link. Keep a buffer for information that arrives later. If the purchase only works when every estimate stays unchanged, the prudent answer is to keep researching rather than force the order to fit."],
["Use the China-warehouse stage as a later control point","LoloBuy says goods are sent to its China warehouse. This creates a useful separation between the seller dispatching an item and any later international delivery. It is not proof that the item is correct or that every later step is settled. Keep the source listing, selected variant and your acceptance note together so that you can compare any available item information against the original choice. Visible images can be helpful for colour, labels, obvious accessories or obvious damage. They cannot reliably prove fabric composition, comfort, durability, authenticity or an eventual delivery outcome. Let the evidence support only the conclusion it can actually support."],
["Create a research row that can survive a second look","The best spreadsheet entry is not the shortest link. It is a compact record another person — or future you — can understand. Keep the original URL, product name, selected variant, displayed price and currency, seller, date checked, measurement notes, visible-image notes and a status. Add a separate column for unresolved risk. Examples include an unclear size chart, a title that conflicts with the specification, promotional images with no item-specific view, or a price that seems unusually low for the stated configuration. Recording uncertainty is not clutter. It prevents an interesting discovery from becoming an accidental recommendation with all the important conditions removed."],
["Ask questions that can produce usable evidence","Broad questions such as ‘is this good?’ rarely create a decision. Replace them with questions tied to a fact: what material is stated, which measurement belongs to the selected size, is a named accessory included, or is the current option still shown on the listing? Put the answer and its date next to the link. A response about one product, seller, parcel or destination should not be copied as a rule for every other case. The value of a question is not that it sounds reassuring. Its value is that it narrows a specific uncertainty and can be rechecked when the listing changes."],
["Keep shipping, customs and authenticity claims proportionate","No independent buying guide can promise a delivery date, a customs decision, a duty amount or authenticity. These outcomes depend on factors outside a static article: the parcel, carrier, destination, local rules, paperwork and later events. Similarly, an image is evidence of what is visible in that image; it is not certification of unseen quality or origin. Write and read advice in conditional terms: check, compare, confirm, record and allow for change. This produces fewer dramatic claims, but much better decisions. It also keeps an article useful when operational details change after publication."],
["Use search to form a shortlist, not to skip verification","FindSpreadsheet can be useful for continuing product research because it helps organize and search ideas. Use queries that describe something testable: dimensions, material, plug type, selected colour, weight or a style plus a size-chart term. The result should be a shortlist of links to examine, not a conclusion that an item is suitable. Go back to the current source listing for the final comparison. A good research page earns its value by helping the reader ask a better next question, not by pretending that a search result is a complete product inspection."],
["Compare two listings feature by feature","When two offers look similar, avoid a quick judgement based only on the first price you notice. Place the exact chosen options side by side. Compare measurements, material wording, images that are actually visible, stated accessories, seller information on the live page and any change or return information that is currently displayed. Mark missing details rather than filling gaps with an assumption. A lower price can reflect a different version, a different quantity, an omitted accessory or a different measurement standard. The goal is not to crown a universal best link. It is to produce a short list where the trade-offs are visible and explainable."],
["Date every useful fact and label the gaps","Listings and options change, and a spreadsheet does not update by itself. Put a date beside each observation and revisit important rows before you act. If a photo is promotional rather than item-specific, call it promotional. If a measurement is not provided, mark it missing. If an amount or delivery step cannot be known yet, call it an estimate or an open question. This level of honesty improves the page for people and for search because it gives readers a clear next action. Strong guidance is not a long sequence of certainties; it is a transparent distinction between what is known, what is shown and what remains to be checked."],
["Decide in small steps instead of one large leap","A careful order has several easy stopping points. First, decide whether the live product page meets your need. Next, decide whether the exact option is clear. Then decide what would make you abandon the item: a wrong measurement, a missing accessory, an unexplained price change or insufficient visible evidence. Only after these answers are written down should you move on with an order flow. The warehouse stage can serve as a later comparison point, but it cannot replace the first product research. Small decisions make it easier to stop early when the evidence no longer supports buying."],
["Match the strength of your language to the strength of evidence","A current listing can support a statement about its displayed title, option, price or image. A public platform homepage can support a statement about the service it describes. Neither one independently proves how every seller, parcel, route or country will behave in the future. This is why this guide avoids words such as guaranteed, safest, cheapest and fastest. Those labels may attract clicks, but they do not help someone decide whether a specific item is right for them. Specific, dated observations are more useful than broad praise because the reader can see exactly what must be confirmed before paying."],
["Set a budget ceiling and a simple exit rule","Write down the highest total you are prepared to accept and the change that would make you stop. Keep the displayed item price, your ceiling and open questions together. Do not let an appealing product price erase the uncertainty that remains about later parts of an order. If a later quote, detail or option exceeds the rule you set, you have a reasoned answer already: do not continue. This is not a claim about any one platform’s fees. It is a practical buying habit that protects against making a pressured decision after you have already invested time in a link."],
["Why this approach is useful for real buyers","This method is designed to be calm rather than impressive. Verify the live listing, record the chosen option, separate facts from estimates, identify the next evidence needed and decide where you will stop. It works whether you are checking one product or building a larger shortlist. It also makes later changes easier to understand because your original facts are still visible. The point is not to suggest that every purchase is risky. The point is to make uncertainty visible early, when changing your mind costs almost nothing."],
["A final pre-order checklist","Before you submit a product link, reopen the current listing and check the exact variant. Record the displayed price without treating it as a final total. Keep your budget ceiling and acceptance rule visible. Save the source URL, seller, date, measurements and any unanswered questions. If later evidence becomes available at the China-warehouse stage, compare it with the note you made before the order. The platform facts used here come only from LoloBuy’s public homepage: purchase assistance for Chinese online and offline channels, a pasted product link to start an order, and goods sent to a China warehouse. This independent guide does not process orders, payments or claims."]
];
